# Optimal Complexity

## Time Complexity

We visit every element exactly once.

```
Rows = m
Columns = n
```

So,

```
Time = O(m × n)
```

No dependence on `k` after reducing it with modulo.

---

## Space Complexity

We create a new answer matrix containing all elements.

```
Answer matrix = m × n
```

Therefore,

```
Space = O(m × n)
```

---

# Complexity Comparison

| Approach    | Time Complexity  | Space Complexity |
| ----------- | ---------------- | ---------------- |
| Brute Force | **O(k × m × n)** | **O(m)**         |
| Optimal     | **O(m × n)**     | **O(m × n)**     |

### Why is the optimal approach better?

* The brute-force method performs the entire shift process `k` times, so its running time grows linearly with `k`.
* The optimal method computes the final destination of each element directly using index mapping, so it processes each element only once regardless of `k`.
* Although the optimal method uses more extra memory (`O(m × n)`), it is significantly faster when `k` is large.
